{
"tag": "main",
"children": [
{
"tag": "div",
"children": [
{
"tag": "p",
"children": [
"Taxes under the Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA) are composed of the old-age, survivors, and disability insurance taxes, also known as Social Security taxes, and the hospital insurance taxes, also known as Medicare taxes. Different rates apply for these taxes."
]
},
{
"tag": "h2",
"children": [
"Social Security and Medicare withholding rates"
]
},
{
"tag": "p",
"children": [
"The current tax rate for Social Security is 6.2% for the employer and 6.2% for the employee, or 12.4% total. The current rate for Medicare is 1.45% for the employer and 1.45% for the employee, or 2.9% total. Refer to",
{
"tag": "a",
"href": "/forms-pubs/about-publication-15",
"children": [
"Publication 15 (Circular E), Employer's Tax Guide"
]
},
"for more information."
]
},
{
"tag": "h2",
"children": [
"Additional Medicare tax withholding rate"
]
},
{
"tag": "p",
"children": [
"Additional Medicare tax applies to an individual's Medicare wages that exceed a threshold amount based on the taxpayer's filing status. Employers are responsible for withholding the 0.9% Additional Medicare tax on an individual's wages paid in excess of $200,000 in a calendar year, without regard to filing status. An employer is required to begin withholding Additional Medicare tax in the pay period in which it pays wages in excess of $200,000 to an employee and continue to withhold it each pay period until the end of the calendar year. There's no employer match for Additional Medicare tax. For more information, see the",
{
"tag": "a",
"href": "/forms-pubs/about-form-8959",
"children": [
"Instructions for Form 8959"
]
},
"and",
{
"tag": "a",
"href": "/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/questions-and-answers-for-the-additional-medicare-tax",
"children": [
"Questions and answers for the Additional Medicare Tax"
]
},
"."
]
},
{
"tag": "h2",
"children": [
"Wage base limits"
]
},
{
"tag": "p",
"children": [
"Only the Social Security tax has a wage base limit. The wage base limit is the maximum wage that's subject to the tax for that year. For earnings in 2026, this base limit is $184,500. Refer to \"What's New\" in",
{
"tag": "a",
"href": "/forms-pubs/about-publication-15",
"children": [
"Publication 15"
]
},
"for the current wage limit for Social Security wages."
]
},
{
"tag": "p",
"children": [
"There's no wage base limit for Medicare tax. All covered wages are subject to Medicare tax."
]
}
]
}
]
}
]
}
]
